WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.509
Space; I’m not sure if you’ve noticed,
but it’s pretty big.

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:10.990
So, when you’re choosing how to get somewhere,
your choice of transport is rather important.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:17.770
For example, if you wanted to drive the 400,000
km to the moon, it would take you about six

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:21.060
months, so long as space traffic was okay.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:27.160
In August, they announced the discovery of
a new planet orbiting our nearest neighbouring

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.830
star, Proxima Centuari, that has the possibility
of being habitable.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:39.199
The big question is; can we realistically
get there and when we do will it be made of

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:40.199
cheese?

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:43.280
Because as tasty as that would be, it’s
not very practical.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.300
Proxima Centuari is just over 4 light years
away, which is nothing relative to the size

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.800
of the universe.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:57.690
However, the fastest spacecraft we’ve ever
sent out Voyager 1, has only covered 1/600th

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.769
of a light-year in 30 years.

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:06.750
At this rate it would take us over 80,000
years to reach Proxima Centauri.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.409
So quite a long journey…

00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:13.250
no Dave, we’re not there yet, go play another
8000 hours of No Man’s Sky.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.759
Oh it’s boring you say, I did tell you to
bring MGS V, but oh no!

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:20.780
But the biggest problem of all is fuel.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.780
There is no way we could carry enough mass
for regular rocket propulsion so what are

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.170
the other options?

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:34.719
How do you make a futuristic space drive?

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:38.700
The first, very elegant option is a sail.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.990
Obviously there is no air out there in vacuum
of space but it’s certainly not empty, there

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.329
are all kinds of particles and waves shooting
around.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:49.829
A solar sail works through solar radiation
pressure.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:56.090
When a light particle, a photon, hits a mirrored
surface, it reflects but the mirror gets a

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:02.020
tiny backwards reaction force, like if you
threw a tennis ball at an elephant when it

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.159
was sitting on the ice, the elephant would
slide away ever so slightly.

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:09.050
I’m not sure why you would be doing this…
but let your imagination go wild.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.100
It was Johannes Kepler who first noticed this
phenomenon in 1610 when he saw that all comet

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:21.130
tails pointed away from the sun, no matter
where in the solar system they came from.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:27.360
The force behind all these particles really
adds up.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.960
Any mission to Mars would need to account
for a few thousand miles of displacement thanks

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.470
to this photon wind.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:40.040
The IKAROS was launched in May 2010 and using
a super thin sail, 7.5-micrometres thick and

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:51.560
14 by 14 meters square, it reached Venus in
6 months, passing by at almost 400 km/h.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.790
Since it will keep accelerating, it’s probably
5 or 6 times that speed now as it carries

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.420
on out into space.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.810
Photons are not the only part of the wind
we could use though since there are proposals

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:08.730
for magnetic sails and electric sails that
would harness the charged particles emitted

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:09.840
by the Sun.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.831
Similarly to the solar sail, they could use
the particle reflections from a magnetic or

00:03:14.831 --> 00:03:17.460
electric field to gain momentum.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:23.350
NASA is currently working on an E-sail idea
that would have around 20 wires sticking out

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.600
of a central hub.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.710
The wires would be just a millimeter thick
but almost 20 km long and the sail would spin

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.190
to keep them all stretched out.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:37.630
Like a gigantic spider web propelling the
spacecraft along, as terrifying as that would

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:43.290
be for arachnophobes, it sure makes for a
really futuristic looking space vessel.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:48.950
Sure a sail is basically free power but by
the time you’ve blown your way across the

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:54.330
high seas from Dublin to New York, you’re
definitely too late for check in and you may

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.130
have lost a leg to sharks.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:01.900
So we might need something a little bit faster
in space.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:08.860
Introducing ion thrusters; ions are atoms
or molecules that have a positive or negative

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:14.230
charge because their number of protons and
electrons don’t match as they do in a regular

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:16.380
atom or molecule.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.660
Most ion drives involve bombarding the fuel
with electrons, so to knock free other electrons

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.400
and create a plasma of positive ions and free
electrons.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.880
The plasma is like a gas but it can be controlled
by an electric field so the drive accelerates

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.410
this plasma out the exhaust to create thrust.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:40.340
It’s nowhere near the power of a rocket
drive, you wouldn’t even be able to push

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:45.000
over a mouse, let alone launch a spacecraft
within Earth’s atmostphere.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:51.240
Deep Space 1 launched in 1998 and it had an
ion drive but its thrust was about the same

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.470
strength as the downward force exerted by
earth’s gravity on one sheet of paper.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.550
Scotty, engage warp drive A4.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:04.770
The Dawn probe was sent out to explore Vesta
and then Ceres, an asteroid and a dwarf planet

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.600
out in the asteroid belt.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:13.650
Its ion drive used just 385 kg of xenon, which
is the normal fuel, to increase its velocity

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.210
by 10 kilometres a second.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.370
So it all adds up, but we’re still a few
years off being able to use ion thrusters

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.139
to travel vast distances.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:28.280
But an increasingly more exciting prospect
is the emergence of the EM drive.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.980
British scientist, Roger Shawyer, designed
the drive in 2001 and it appears to create

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:39.760
thrust without releasing any propellant, something
we had thought was impossible.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:46.050
Well, most scientists still do believe it’s
impossible and have treated it highly suspiciously.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.230
The way it works is…

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:52.370
I’m going to be honest here, I don’t understand
this one, because it seems to break physics

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:57.460
so clearly that I think Roger Shawyer has
made a deal with Space Satan.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.389
But, here’s the general overview.

00:06:00.389 --> 00:06:03.830
The Em drive would use solar power to create
microwaves.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:08.880
These microwaves go into a blunted cone chamber
called a resonant cavity thruster.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.811
The microwaves bounce off the walls and exert
a larger force on the wide end than on the

00:06:12.811 --> 00:06:15.540
narrow end, creating thrust.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:23.080
Why it does this is the big argument and some
say it’s down to an as-yet-unproven concept

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:28.850
called Unruh radiation, which is predicted
by Einstein’s General Relativity but hasn’t

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.010
been observed so far.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:35.510
Trying to understand this is like being 4
years old and listening to your parents spell

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.130
things they don’t want you hear.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.280
What is this B-E-D and why am I going?

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.280
Is it good?

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.389
Can I take teddy?

00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:45.920
Does it obey Newton’s Second Law?

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.730
There are now plans to soon send an Em Drive
into space to see if it really does work up

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:56.460
there in the vacuum so we can argue about
something else instead.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.230
Roger Shawyer is exactly what you’d expect
a scientist to look like, a disheveled white

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:02.570
haired old man.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:07.960
But occasionally, ideas come from much more
unexpected places.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:14.169
At just 19, Egyptian physicist Aisha Mustafa
proposed an idea for a drive that would use

00:07:14.169 --> 00:07:17.790
the very vacuum of space itself to provide
thrust.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:21.790
Now, a vacuum is not as empty as we initially
believed.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.330
Every part of space is filled with virtual
particles.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:30.320
Despite the name, they do exist, just for
incredibly tiny periods of time.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:37.520
In 1948, Hendrik Casimir predicted that these
virtual particles could result in real physical

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:38.710
forces.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:44.380
The way to observe them is to put two reflective
mirrors just nanometers apart, in a vacuum.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:49.440
Their close proximity means that you limit
the ways electromagnetic vibration can happen

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.760
in this space, compared to the space on the
outside of the two mirrors, where there are

00:07:54.760 --> 00:08:00.820
more modes of vibration so you get more pressure
on the outside and a force is created, pushing

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:01.950
the mirrors together.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.070
It’s called the static Casimir Effect.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:12.200
In 1970, Gerald Moore came up with the dynamic
Casimir Effect, that involved moving this

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:16.350
pair of mirrors at very high speed, close
to light speed.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:22.570
The field would not have time to adjust at
this velocity and it could produce real photons.

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.930
This is where the thrust comes from in Mustafa’s
drive.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:30.830
So if you run around for a few hours wrapped
in two layers of shiny tin foil, you never

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.449
know, you might create enough thrust to go
into space.

00:08:34.449 --> 00:08:39.399
These are just a few of the leading ideas
for futuristic space drives, there are plenty

00:08:39.399 --> 00:08:40.849
of others banging around.

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:46.319
There’s the Bussard ramjet which would use
thousand-kilometer-wide fields to scoop up

00:08:46.319 --> 00:08:50.750
all the hydrogen atoms that are scattered
through space, before compressing them enough

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.870
so that you get nuclear fusion.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:09:00.290
There’s the proposed Alcubierre drive which
would contract space in front of it and expand

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:04.980
space behind it, possibly moving faster than
the speed of light.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.920
It’s actually mathematically possible but
that doesn’t mean it’s going to happen.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.290
It would require “exotic matter”, and
not the kind that you find under a teenage

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:15.879
boy’s bed.

00:09:15.879 --> 00:09:21.139
Of course, scientists are a complicated bunch
and maybe they have overlooked an obvious

00:09:21.139 --> 00:09:26.040
solution; if mankind really want to travel
to the stars, perhaps we just all need to

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.360
get out and push.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:32.420
But seriously, it’s only a matter of time
before we can travel as fast or even faster

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.329
than the speed of light, it will happen.

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:40.129
The solution could come from a leading scientist
or one of those annoyingly genius-level kids

00:09:40.129 --> 00:09:41.930
at a school science fair.

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:48.260
When it does happen we will have a whole universe
to explore, our very own No Man’s Sky, lets

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.800
just hope the real version is more interesting.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:10:04.589
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

